-## Data
+**Aggregate welfare bounds** (2016–2017):
+- Contemporaneous: total health expenditure reduction ≈$41M; public-share lower bound $28.6M (respiratory $18.4M + cardiovascular $10.2M).
+- Dynamic: total ≈$52M; public lower bound $36.7M.
+- System cost ≈$4M (Tables A12–A13).
+- **BCR: 7.1:1** (contemporaneous); **9.2:1** (dynamic).
+- **Net benefit: $24.6M** ($32.7M dynamic).
 
-Health spending is a 10% random sample of the National Health Insurance Service (NHIS) for seven major South Korean cities (Seoul, Busan, Daegu, Daejeon, Incheon, Gwangju, Ulsan), covering ~2.3 million individuals over 2016–2017 (source: Anderson_etal_2022_NBER.pdf). Daily per-capita spending is broken out by disease (ICD-10 "I" cardiovascular, "J" respiratory) and age (minors 0–19, adults 20–64, older adults 65+). The main analysis **excludes** inpatient hospitalizations (because the lag between symptom onset and inpatient admission is typically a week or more and would inject noise into a daily panel) and excludes outpatient visits to tertiary general hospitals (which require referrals); robustness adds them back in (source: Anderson_etal_2022_NBER.pdf).
-
-The project's MedPAR-based JMP design takes the opposite choice: it analyzes daily inpatient admissions because the legacy Deryugina et al. (2019) ozone-and-mortality literature has established that daily inpatient counts respond detectably to acute ozone exposure at the day level. The Anderson et al. exclusion is specific to the Korean PM-alert RD context, not a general design principle.
-
-The out-of-pocket payment ratio ranges from 10–50% across institution types, with an average of ~30% OOP / ~70% public coverage — the "70%" the welfare bound applies to FRD coefficients (source: Anderson_etal_2022_NBER.pdf, Tables A3 and 2).
-
-Alert data come from the Korea Environment Corporation (KECO) website. Operating-cost data come from municipal environmental-expenditure reports (Appendix Tables A12 and A13). NAVER search-engine counts provide independent behavioral evidence of alert salience (Figure 2).
-
-## Headline Results
-
-Fuzzy-RD 2SLS estimates of the change in per-capita daily health expenditures (US cents) due to an alert issuance at bandwidth $h=20$ (source: Anderson_etal_2022_NBER.pdf, Tables 4 and 5):
-
